{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794832624,"nonce":"AA5xwxkM8Fx9gzjcnWTYM9EB4+E=","title":"Load hxcpp CFFI libraries (.ndll) on HashLink and Ash through a cffi.hdll","message":"Goal: a program on HashLink or Ash calls an existing hxcpp CFFI library (DEFINE_PRIM / DEFINE_PRIME, an .ndll) without the library being rebuilt or rewritten. The same .ndll keeps working on hxcpp.\n\nWhy it can work. The Ash session checked hxcpp git's include/hx CFFI.h, CFFIAPI.h and CFFILoader.h, and Ash's std/src/hl_compat.rs and gc.rs. `value` is an opaque `struct _value*`. Every access goes through the roughly 95 functions CFFIAPI.h names; only HXCPP_NATIVE_CFFI_VALUE, hxcpp-internal, makes it hx::Object*. Each ndll keeps a static ResolveProc, set through the exported hx_set_loader or found with dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, \"hx_cffi\"). Calling hx_set_loader per ndll is the controlled way. Functions resolve lazily. A few, such as val_is_buffer, alloc_empty_string and the string helpers, fall back to built-in defaults when the resolver returns null. val_array_bool/int/double/float/value may return null. Not yet read: CFFIPrime.h (Prime's signature strings).\n\nDesign (agreed in principle with the Ash session, 2026-11-16):\n- One cffi.hdll, written on HashLink's public C API, so it loads unchanged on stock HashLink and on Ash. ash_std already exports hl_alloc_dynamic, hl_alloc_dynobj, hl_dyn_get/set*, hl_hash_utf8, hl_dyn_call, hl_throw, hl_add_root/remove_root, hl_register_thread/unregister_thread, hl_blocking, hl_gc_alloc_gen, hl_make_dyn and hl_to/from_utf8.\n- A Haxe loader shim, so cpp.Lib.load and cpp.Prime.load work under -hl. It calls generic natives: cffi_load(path), cffi_resolve(lib, name, nargs) and cffi_call(fn, NativeArray\u003cDynamic\u003e).\n- Both live in hxn's repo and haxelib. Stock HashLink can only be tested on Linux.\n\nValue model:\n- Ints, floats, bools, null and strings become boxed vdynamic, with null as NULL. val_int and val_float convert by tag, since HL boxes I32, F32, F64, I64 and bool separately.\n- val_id is hl_hash_utf8. val_field and alloc_field go through hl_dyn_getp/setp. alloc_empty_object is hl_alloc_dynobj. Fields of Haxe classes need @:keep.\n- Strings are UTF-16 in HL, so val_string and val_wstring return converted copies kept alive for the call. wchar_t is 4 bytes on macOS and Linux.\n- Arrays: the generic val_array_i/set_i/push path only, through closures the Haxe shim hands over at init (new, get, set, length, push). Every val_array_\u003ctype\u003e pointer function returns NULL. That also avoids writes that skip Ash's generational write barrier.\n- Abstracts: one HABSTRACT hl_type per kind. kind_share is a name table under a mutex. val_gc finalizers mean every abstract is allocated as a finalizable block (hl_gc_alloc_gen).\n\nGC, threads and exceptions:\n- alloc_root is a malloc'd slot plus hl_add_root. gc_set_top_of_stack is hl_register_thread, and gc_enter/exit_blocking is hl_blocking.\n- A native thread calling val_call into a Haxe closure needs a probe in each Ash mode: hybrid, JIT and interpreter.\n- val_throw and hx_fail throw a C++ exception of the hdll's own type. The name__n trampoline catches it and calls hl_throw after the ndll's frames are gone. Calling hl_throw (Ash's trap longjmp) inside ndll frames would skip C++ destructors.\n\nAsh-side additions it would need (not yet exported, from a quick scan): hl_field_name (for val_field_name and val_iter_fields), hl_dyn_call_safe, hl_alloc_obj, hl_alloc_virtual and hl_alloc_enum. Also adding cffi.hdll to Ash's hdll tests in CI.\n\nScope: native first. A prebuilt .ndll is machine code, so wasm means rebuilding the library with -DSTATIC_LINK against a wasm cffi runtime that defines the val_* symbols and hx_register_prim. Decide that after the test ndll exists. Out of scope: hxcpp libraries made of C++ externs (@:native, @:include, untyped __cpp__); rebind those with hxn import.\n\nProof, in order:\n1. A test ndll of about 100 lines, covering UTF-8 strings, ints against floats, object fields, generic arrays, kind_share across two ndlls, a finalizer, alloc_root across a forced collection, val_call1 from the main thread and from a library thread in each Ash mode, and val_throw.\n2. hxcpp's own project/libs sqlite, compared against Ash's crates/ash_hdll_sqlite.\nLime's lime.ndll is the wrong target: Lime 8 ships a lime.hdll for HashLink, and the legacy ndll uses much of the API.","files":null}]}